  {
   "t": 1791260567.719328,
   "kind": "assistant_text",
   "text": "## Summary\n\n**Search process:**\n1. Declared three stopping triggers up front: `best_so_far_above` (1.2) \u2192 stop, `failures_at_least` (4) \u2192 stop, `last_gain_at_most` (0.02) \u2192 stop.\n2. Skipped `pick_prior` \u2014 I had no feature-specific belief independent of this dataset, so I let the empirical search (`init`) do the discovery rather than anchor on an unjustified guess.\n3. Called `init`, which anchored on the single best feature across 80 candidates (40 features \u00d7 long/short): feature **36, short**, in-sample annualized Sharpe **1.6068**.\n4. Checked my `best_so_far_above(1.2)` trigger \u2014 it fired immediately, since 1.6068 > 1.2. Per the rules, once a declared trigger fires the harness won't proceed further until I act on it, so I called `stop` on that trigger, which executed and locked in this single-feature specification.\n\n**Final specification:** Equal-weight (trivially, single-feature) combination: **feature 36, short**.\n\n**Submission:** \n- Spec: `[36-]`\n- In-sample Sharpe: 1.6068\n- Predicted OOS Sharpe: **mean 0.55, sd 0.50**\n\n**Reasoning on the prediction:** A Sharpe of 1.6 selected as the *maximum* over 80 sign/feature combinations is subject to meaningful selection bias \u2014 the in-sample figure overstates the true edge. I shrunk my central estimate to roughly a third of the in-sample value and used a wide standard deviation (0.5) to reflect genuine uncertainty: it's plausible the feature captures a real, persistent anomaly that partially survives the stated transaction costs and borrow fees out-of-sample, but it's also plausible much of the in-sample Sharpe is noise from searching across many candidates."
  },
